WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:04.310
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.310 --> 00:00:09.620
In this series, we often talk about smoke
and fluid simulations, and sometimes, the

00:00:09.620 --> 00:00:14.580
examples showcase a beautiful smoke plume,
but not much else.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:19.650
However, in real production environments,
these simulations often involve complex scenes

00:00:19.650 --> 00:00:25.130
with many objects that interact with each
other, and therein lies the problem.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:29.820
Computing these interactions is called coupling,
and it is very difficult to get right, but

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:33.330
is necessary for many of the scenes you will
see throughout this video.

00:00:33.330 --> 00:00:38.980
This new graphics paper builds on a technique
called the Lattice Boltzmann Method and promises

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:41.520
a better way to compute this two-way coupling.

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:47.100
For instance, in this simulation, two-way
coupling is required to compute how this fiery

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:49.989
smoke trail propels the rocket upward.

00:00:49.989 --> 00:00:56.309
So, coupling means interaction between different
kinds of objects, but what about the two-way

00:00:56.309 --> 00:00:57.309
part?

00:00:57.309 --> 00:00:58.500
What does that mean exactly?

00:00:58.500 --> 00:01:02.489
Well, first, let’s have a look at one way
coupling.

00:01:02.489 --> 00:01:07.860
As the box moves here, it has an effect on
the smoke plume around it.

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:12.950
This example also showcases one-way coupling,
where the falling plate stirs up the smoke

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:14.060
around it.

00:01:14.060 --> 00:01:18.170
The parts with the higher Reynolds numbers
showcase more turbulent flows.

00:01:18.170 --> 00:01:22.760
Typically, that’s the real good stuff if
you ask me.

00:01:22.760 --> 00:01:24.980
And now, on to two-way coupling.

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:30.730
In this case, similarly to the previous ones,
the boxes are allowed to move the smoke, but

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:36.420
the added two-way coupling part means that
now, the smoke is also allowed to blow away

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:37.450
the boxes.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.700
What’s more, the vortices here on the right
were even able to suspend the red box in the

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.340
air for a few seconds.

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:48.640
An excellent demonstration of a beautiful
phenomenon.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.160
Now let’s look at the previous example with
the dropping plate and see what happens.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:02:00.990
Yes, indeed, as the plate drops, it moves
the smoke, and as the smoke moves, it also

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:03.920
blows away the boxes.

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:05.740
Woo-hoo!

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.750
Due to improvements in the coupling computation,
it also simulates these kinds of vortices

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:13.750
much more realistically than previous works.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:17.250
Just look at all this magnificent progress
in just 2 years.

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:20.780
So, what else can we do with all this?

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:24.480
What are some typical scenarios that require
accurate two-way coupling?

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:31.950
Well, for instance, it can perform an incredible
tornado simulation, that you see here, and

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:36.030
there is an alternative view where we only
see the objects moving about.

00:02:36.030 --> 00:02:42.410
So, all this looks good, but really, how do
we know how accurate this technique is?

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:47.920
And now comes my favorite part, and this is
when we let reality be our judge and compare

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:51.830
the simulation results with real-world experiments.

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:56.790
Hold on to your papers while you observe the
real experiment here on the left.

00:02:56.790 --> 00:03:01.330
And now, the algorithmic reproduction of the
same scene here.

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:02.820
How close are they?

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.760
Goodness…very, very close.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:10.260
I will stop the footage at different times
so we can both evaluate it better.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.140
Love it.

00:03:12.140 --> 00:03:18.540
The technique can also undergo the wind tunnel
test, here is the real footage, and here is

00:03:18.540 --> 00:03:21.470
the simulation.

00:03:21.470 --> 00:03:26.220
And it is truly remarkable how close this
is able to match it, and I was wondering that

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:30.709
even though as someone who has been doing
fluids for a while now, if someone cropped

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:35.500
this part of the image and told me that it
is real-world footage, I would have believed

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:36.900
it in a second.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:38.490
Absolute insanity.

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:42.870
So, how much do we have to wait to compute
a simulation like this?

00:03:42.870 --> 00:03:48.290
Well, great news, it uses your graphics card,
which is typically the case for the more rudimentary

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:53.280
fluid simulation algorithms out there, but
the more elaborate ones typically don’t

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:58.270
support it, or at least, not without a fair
amount of additional effort.

00:03:58.270 --> 00:04:04.340
The quickest example was this, as it was simulated
in less than 6 seconds, which I find to be

00:04:04.340 --> 00:04:05.340
mind blowing.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:11.420
A smoke simulation with box movements in a
few seconds, I am truly out of words.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:17.010
The rocket launch scene took the longest with
16 hours, while the falling plate example

00:04:17.010 --> 00:04:23.090
with the strong draft that threw the boxes
around was 4.5 hours of computation time.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:28.990
The results depend greatly on delta t, which
is the size of time steps, or in others words,

00:04:28.990 --> 00:04:34.020
in how small increments we can advance time
when creating these simulations to make sure

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:36.710
we don’t miss any important interactions.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:41.600
You see here that in the rocket example, we
have to simulate roughly a hundred thousand

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:45.030
steps for every second of video footage.

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.150
No wonder it takes so long!

00:04:47.150 --> 00:04:51.970
We have an easier time with this scene where
these time steps can be 50 times larger without

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.150
losing any detail, and hence, it goes much
faster.

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:01.410
The grid resolution also matters a great deal,
which specifies how many spatial points the

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:03.570
simulation has to take place in.

00:05:03.570 --> 00:05:08.820
The higher the resolution the grid, the larger
region we can cover, and the more details

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:10.020
we can simulate.

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:14.840
As most research works, this technique doesn’t
come without limitations, however.

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.830
It is less accurate if we have simulations
involving thin rods and shells and typically

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:24.780
uses two to three times more memory than a
typical simulator program.

00:05:24.780 --> 00:05:29.960
If these are the only tradeoffs to create
all this marvelous footage, sign me up this

00:05:29.960 --> 00:05:31.010
very second!

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:35.550
Overall, this paper is extraordinarily well
written and presented, and of course, it has

00:05:35.550 --> 00:05:40.110
been accepted to the SIGGRAPH conference,
one of the most prestigious scientific venues

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:41.880
in computer graphics research.

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:46.370
Huge congratulations to the authors, and if
you wish to see something beautiful today,

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:50.070
make sure to have a look at the paper itself
in the video description.

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:53.210
Truly stunning work.

00:05:53.210 --> 00:06:43.030
What a time to be alive!

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:46.370
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

